WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.110
Narrator: This is Tim Kimmel,

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:14.370
the supervising sound
editor on "Game of Thrones."

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.280
Not only is he the voice behind

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.670
some of the show's
wights and White Walkers,

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.230
but through a process
of mixing and layering,

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:23.390
he helped create the stuff of nightmares

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.307
for the people of Westeros
and viewers at home.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.653
Warning: Spoilers are coming.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.730
Kimmel has been working on the show

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.900
since the start of season three.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.310
In fact, he won an Emmy for his work

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:42.170
in the episode "Hardhome."

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.440
He and the sound team
had to bend a few rules

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.440
to get them to make the
sound in the first place.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.600
For example, creatures
like this with no skin

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.240
and likely no vocal chords
probably shouldn't be able

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.610
to make any sound at all.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.020
- Well, if it comes at you quietly,

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:58.860
it doesn't really have the same impact,

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.070
especially some of these
straight-up skeletons.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:02.260
They don't have vocal chords.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.920
So, you know, we sometimes
would try to lean away

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.920
from the wights that were so
rotted away with no flesh.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.010
We talked about the sounds
that they'd be making.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.320
They'd have vocal chords
that are kind of shredded.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:14.980
Narrator: The sounds they did give them

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.650
came from a variety of places, and Kimmel

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.680
has an especially personal
connection with the wights.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.120
Some of the sounds the wights made

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.787
actually came directly from him.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.930
The sound, as he describes it,

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:31.820
came from the back of his throat.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.390
After enough distorting
in the editing room,

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.473
it sounds more like this.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.120
You could also hear him doing the sound

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.120
of Wun Wun the giant.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.530
- I'll admit, I'm a big metalhead.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.350
I grew up in the '80s, so
it is sort of a metal yell.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.000
It's a (roaring) kind of yell.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.630
Narrator: And with the wight giant,

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.240
they used the same sound elements

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.823
but with a grittier feel to it.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.660
But it's not just Kimmel's voice in there.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.100
If you listen carefully, you
might hear some animal sounds.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.660
Some of the most vicious
sounds coming from the wights

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.660
were actually sounds of dogs

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.100
that were manipulated and tweaked enough

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.850
that you couldn't tell they
were dog sounds originally.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:25.650
And that wight that Jon Snow
smuggled into King's Landing

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.880
to show Cersei in season seven

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.070
was actually making the
sounds of a bear cub.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:33.070
A lot of work happens in postproduction

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:34.737
to make sure this...

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.760
will sound like this.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.640
More on this scene in a bit.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.430
After years of work, many sounds are saved

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:50.300
in a vast digital library and reused.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:53.840
However, the sounds they used
and the sounds the creatures

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.360
make would still change
depending on the scene.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.200
- It has to do with what they
look like, what they're doing.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.600
As they're just walking
or slowly attacking,

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.180
they don't make much
sounds, but as they start

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.630
to really attack, the
more aggressive ones,

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:09.060
we'll go with a more aggressive sound.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.780
A lot of it has to be story-driven.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.450
If there's one that's right
on top of a main character

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.110
that you're worried is gonna kill them,

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.770
we'll find the most
aggressive one we can find

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.120
and really make the viewer
worry, "Oh no, this is it."

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.570
It helps compel that
story a little bit more.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:24.280
Narrator: The more
aggressive the creatures,

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:27.210
the more aggressive the
sounds they use will get.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.907
You can't talk about
the sounds of the wights

00:03:28.907 --> 00:03:32.070
and White Walkers without
discussing how they move.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.190
Much of the movements
are done through Foley,

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.550
a process in which real objects are used

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:38.280
to create sounds of living creatures

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.930
interacting with the world.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.880
When it comes to their
movement, they start bare bones,

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.860
literally, as a lot of the
wights are nothing but skeletons.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.150
They used real bones and ceramic objects,

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:52.840
but you'll also hear a lot
of other sounds at play

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.290
in any given scenes where

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:57.360
the undead army is scurrying around.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:58.910
- A lot of those wights were soldiers,

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:00.910
and they'd have some armor
still hanging off of them

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:02.050
or some leather hanging off of them,

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:05.700
so we also incorporate that
and incorporate some of the

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.390
flesh sounds a little bit
and a little bit of cloth.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:12.600
Just so it's not just bones, just to,

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.070
it gives it a little more depth.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.100
Narrator: The further
the show has moved along,

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.270
the more wights they've had to deal with.

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:20.640
When Kimmel first started
working on the show,

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.710
they were making sound for
just a handful of them.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:26.180
On the other hand, the
Battle of Winterfell

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.300
saw the North face off against an army

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:31.420
of thousands of wights and White Walkers.

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:33.100
During this battle, they were dealing

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.740
with hundreds of different sounds at once.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.070
One of the biggest challenges
was capturing sound

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.240
that really gave the audience the feel

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.407
of the scope of this army.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.490
As the North's army waited
for the undead to approach,

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:53.810
it had to be very quiet at first

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.850
and then get progressively
louder as they crept closer

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.940
and closer to the city's walls.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.270
- You had to get as big as
you can for that first wave,

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.600
so it just feels huge, but
what was tricky in the shot

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.180
was that first wave hits, and then you get

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:09.340
to a reaction shot of the
other part of the army,

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.220
and then there's a second
wave that comes in,

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.310
so if you go too big on the first wave,

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:15.030
you don't feel the second
wave, so you had to find

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:18.840
a way to feel big but still
have room to get even bigger.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.040
Narrator: But surprisingly,
the hardest moments

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.470
for Kimmel and his team
came not when dealing

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:27.520
with armies but when dealing
with a single wight on screen.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:28.750
Back to that scene where Jon

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:30.670
brings a wight into King's Landing.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:32.020
- Usually when these wights are going,

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:33.560
there's a lot of stuff to hide behind.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:34.760
There's other battle things,

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:37.060
and so it's not as out in the open,

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:38.410
so you can kinda do more with it

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:39.870
and get away with little cheats,

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.640
but this thing was on its own.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.473
It needed to be scary.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:48.867
Narrator: The thing that made the wights

00:05:48.867 --> 00:05:51.836
and White Walkers truly
terrifying throughout their run

00:05:51.836 --> 00:05:54.180
wasn't just the sounds they made

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.730
but also the ones they didn't make.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.550
In an early appearance in
season two, you can hear

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.750
a White Walker speak in
Skroth, their native tongue.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:06.500
This was then abandoned
in later appearances.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.410
Kimmel also pointed out
that in early appearances,

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.880
the Night King would open
his mouth and show his teeth.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:18.270
- I think they just found
him more intimidating

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.840
to just be there with a
straight look on his face.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:22.250
He's just standing there confident.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:25.560
You don't know what he's gonna
do or what he's capable of.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.500
Narrator: Sometimes, no sound

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.283
is the scariest sound of all.

